#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199250} :activity/id #uuid "8416c2c4-cff0-5744-94c6-6f83c5fc5709" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050482 :topic/id #uuid "4d32e47e-9f37-5a31-95c8-7aa1fca70d66" :topic/math-academy-id 4132} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199250 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199451 :tutorial/content "Suppose that\n\n$$\\mathbf T: (u,v) \\to \\left(x(u,v),y(u,v)\\right)$$\n\nis a $C^1$ transformation that has no critical points (i.e., it is invertible) inside some region $\\Delta\\subset \\mathbb R^2,$ and that $\\mathbf T(\\Delta) = D,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/66/668b8a0dae060c7929b92c6abe3601b0ede0b8444d92f33f4a8b5627410a4447.png)\n\nWe've already seen how the area of a non-trivial region $D$ can be computed using the change of variables formula: $$\\iint\\limits_{D} \\mathrm d x \\mathrm d y = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\right| \\ \\mathrm d u \\mathrm d v$$\n\nWe can extend this formula to compute the integral of a function $f(x,y)$ over $D,$ as follows:\n\n$$\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d x \\mathrm d y = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(x(u,v), y(u,v)) \\: \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\right| \\ \\mathrm d u \\mathrm d v$$\n\nNote the similarity with the change of variables formula for the integral of a single-variable function $f(x)$ over $D = (a,b){:}$\n\n$$\\int\\limits_D f(x)\\,\\textrm d x = \\int\\limits_{\\Delta} f(x(u))\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d u}\\, \\textrm d u$$\n\nApplying the change of variables formula is similar to when we were restricting our attention to areas in the plane. The only difference now is that we must also express the integrand $f(x,y)$ in terms of $u$ and $v.$\n\nLet's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "2fb63b1e-64c3-58e1-94d8-3d7a87445cd7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4402 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "5a4dcbdc-b666-5ac5-82b5-d1e2d96f958e" :step/math-academy-id 26976 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199251}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199251 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198361 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198235 :question/id #uuid "9d477733-7178-5b88-926f-a457faaf344a" :question/math-academy-id "e-7285"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9fed8ac2-8d21-5bc9-b1cc-df20760cfbfb" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050705}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198948} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198950} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373909} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375103} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375495} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375502} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375509}] :knowledge-point/title "Performing a Change of Variables"} :step/id #uuid "38215948-f2f7-5757-b967-2b0aad077150" :step/math-academy-id 26978 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199273}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199273 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198373 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198015 :question/id #uuid "e8d75369-b381-53d3-b3af-37c6109c15a0" :question/math-academy-id "e-12327"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd546dda-c78f-5367-b03e-9b3acb420040" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198951} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199007} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373930} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373937} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373944} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375516}] :knowledge-point/title "Cases When the Transformation Is Inferred From the Domain"} :step/id #uuid "418d8ff2-cd87-5ef1-b69c-c511c7c5b360" :step/math-academy-id 44170 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199274}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199274 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198374 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198016 :question/id #uuid "1ad4ddff-b776-50d9-b698-c0172ee9d3ab" :question/math-academy-id "e-12328"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b2682751-23e4-5cf2-9a46-82c75765423f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198579} {:db/id #edb/ref 1